  "result": "I wrote the revised `drv.interaction.overtake_pass` record to `record.jsonl`. It hasn't been coded, gated or reviewed yet.\n\n**What failed and why:** with `rel.bearing` masked, the `blind` scenario still came out compliant. A missing value can't make a conjunction true, and the body requires b to be behind a in bearing terms. So the evaluator must be working out the bearing from the kinematics even when the manifest declares it absent; I inferred that, I didn't see it. That fits ontology §7: the relation atoms are \"Computed from kinematics.\" Masking `rel.bearing` can't make this body unknown, so the record had to change rather than the coder's body.\n\n**Changes to the record:**\n- **Ahead / behind:** the contract no longer reads b's position relative to a through `rel.bearing`. It now uses the kinematics that bearing is computed from: the component along a's heading (`kin.heading`) of the vector from a's position to b's position (`kin.position`). Only its sign is read: positive means b is ahead, negative means b is behind. This is the same quantity as the longitudinal part of the bearing, so the notion is unchanged. The element now claims `kin.heading` and `kin.position`; `rel.bearing` is dropped because the body no longer reads it.\n- **`blind` scenario:** it now masks `kin.position` instead of `rel.bearing`. With the positions unobserved, the coder's \"b behind a\" check comes out unknown at the judged observation. Because the headline condition (b behind a) is required, the verdict can't be true.\n- **Contract wording:** I added one closing sentence: where the positions of a and b are unobserved, the concept is unknown. The rest of the contract text and the `act` fields are unchanged. Rule 3 allows the rewording because the concept isn't accepted and nothing accepted depends on it.\n- **Kept as before:** the formals, parameters, the other two elements, and the `holds` and `fails` scenarios.\n\n**For the next coding session:** the ahead/behind test has to be written as arithmetic on the position and heading measures. A body that still reads `rel.bearing` will hit the same failure.\n\n**Separate harness problem:** the coding session's own gate runs reported a different failure, a scope violation. The shell tool writes its output files under `.runtime/tmp` inside the repository tree, and the scope check counts them as changes. Changing the record doesn't fix that; the scope check needs to ignore `.runtime/tmp`, or the scratch directory needs to live outside the repository.",
